A||N||18||jhogans||I found a potential Fiero?||||10
Z||000000||jhogans||10-04-1999||11:14 PM||||You guys know that I've been looking at buying a Fiero.  I found one in my area but it needs some work.  This is the following info:<P>$1600 OBO<BR>87 GT<BR>less than 90000 miles<BR>power windows<BR>power door locks<BR>power mirrors<BR>Tilt<BR>New Clutch  - 1 year old<P>What it Needs:<BR>Paint job<BR>Clutch Master Cylinder<BR>Driver and Passenger Side Mirrors - the originals mirrors were broken by vandals.<P>Q1 - The seller says that the price is negotiable.  Cash on hand has major influence.  How much would should I pay for the above?<P>Q2 - How hard is it to put a new Clutch Master Cylinder in?  How do I install one?  Any sites out there that describe the <BR>procedure in detail?  How much does one cost?<P>Q3 - How much are used power side mirrors going for?  Where can I buy some?<P><BR>Thanks for any info guys.  You have been very helpful.  Thanks alot.||||reg||10
Z||000001||TOM||10-04-1999||11:33 PM||||I can get you the power mirror assys. cheap.||||reg||1
Z||000002||87FieroGT||10-05-1999||01:22 AM||||I would try to offer the guy between $1,000-$1,200. It it going to cost you the other 400-600 or more to fix the things that are wrong with it. I got my 87 GT for $1,700 but it had a less than 70,000 miles on it, a good paint job, but I did have to fix the clutch hydraulics before I could drive it. It does however have some frame damage to the rear which I have yet to get fixed. Make sure you check for that also. The Fiero is pretty bad at being able to tell if it has benn hit or not with the type of body panels it has. You need to get up underneath it to see any damage. Anyway good luck on your new purchase.||||reg||13
Z||000003||Patrick||10-05-1999||01:44 AM||||<BR>A clutch master cylinder is relatively cheap (less than $100(US) I would think) and it is quite easy to install. My concern is that if the clutch is not disengaging properly now, who's to say it's just the master cylinder that's to blame.<P>Changing the clutch master cylinder is one thing, yanking the engine/transaxle out is another. <P>||||reg||4
Z||000004||lowCG||10-05-1999||01:48 AM||||Start out at $900,then when the seller refuses,start to go up gradually,continually pointing out the problems with the car,the age,and so on,until it's yours.The seller probably has been scared of the car/maintenance/comittment and is ready to sell.<BR>Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000005||jhogans||10-06-1999||03:35 PM||||I just got back from looking at the Fiero I was telling you guys about.  That car was in need of some serious help inside and outside of the car.  It was in bad shape.  I guess I will continue my search  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||BlackGT||10-07-1999||02:21 AM||||JHogans,<BR>If this GT is the one me and my girl looked at a few weeks ago(Black exterior grey interior with damage every where!) leave it the hell alone!!! Too much work before you can actually drive the car. The guy is nice enough but I`ll give you some advice I was given when I bought my first car.... if you<BR>can`t test drive..keep walking! That GT has<BR>sat too long and been neglected, don`t fool with it. BTW did you see the last message I<BR>posted to you? If not why don`t you e-mail me<BR>at toddag@excite.com and I`ll help you with your search!!! I live in Atlanta and since I<BR>bought our GT`s all kinds of people tell me <BR>of others...I just haven`t been writing down the info when they tell me, but I will in the future!!! ||||reg||6
Z||000007||batboy||10-07-1999||03:21 AM||||I've read Fiero ads in the paper that sounded good and then called them on the phone. After talking with them I was nearly convinced this was the ultimate Fiero deal. Wonderful car at a low price. But... go look at it and suddenly it seems like they were talking about a totally different car. Makes me mad that they were dishonest and wasted my time. Once I pulled into a driveway, took one look at the car, didn't even turn off my car, just backed right out and got away fast.<P>Recently, I went to look at a 1984 Fiero with a "fresh paint job" and the owner was moving and "had to sell it" for a low price of $1,500. Ended up that the car was half disassembled (he forgot to tell me that on the phone he said) and the paint job was a spray can number that looked like crap (overspray on all the windows, mega runs, debris and bugs stuck to the paint, etc.). I told him that I wasn't interested and began walked back to my own Fiero in disgust. He had the nerve to follow me and tell me that he was willing to sacrifice it for a mere $1,000. I turned around and said, "Buddy, I wouldn't buy that car for $200. He sure looked shocked.<P>The moral is to keep looking. Before I bought my last 85 GT, I looked at a dozen Fieros at least. Almost all of them had saggy headliners, worn out driver's seat, a non-working A/C system. Most of the time they'll tell you, "sure the air works, it just needs recharged is all" which means the compressor seals are shot at the very least. Usually just as you are about to give up looking, a great deal pops up, so be patient.||||reg||1
Z||000008||RFMike||10-07-1999||08:03 AM||||One way to get a jump on everyone else that is looking is to log on to the various online car selling services on Saturday morning and afternoon.  They list what is going to be advertised in Sunday's car want ads, so you can beat everyone who is only using the newspaper for cars that are newly listed on Sunday.  It works.  When I bought my car, I called the owner at 1:00 p.m. Saturday afternoon.  He was shocked that I had the info.  I told him it was on the internet, but he didn't believe me at first, then realized I was telling him the truth.<BR>If you've never looked at these services, try it.  Some you can specify the location, age of the car, key words such as GT, etc.<BR>It's been quite a while since I've done a car search.  I had opinions on them but I don't remember which I thought was best.  I know i was looking at cars.com, traderonline.com, autotraderonline.com.  There are others.  Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000009||jhogans||10-07-1999||08:16 AM||||Thanks for all of the info guys.  I will keep looking especially over the internet.  RFMike,  that is a really good idea.  I will give it a try this week.<P>Black Gt,<P>I tried to send mail to your email address, toddag@excite.com, but it get returned to me with an error that said, User Unknown.  Send me a test email at jhogans@mindspring.com.  I would really very appreciative if you could throw some bones my way when you catch wind of some Fiero's for sale.  That would really be helpful.||||reg||1
Z||000010||batboy||10-07-1999||09:38 PM||||Here is another source that advertises Fieros for sell. You have to scroll a long ways down towards the bottom in order to reach the latest listings (there's about a year and a half worth of ads).<P> <A HREF="http://www.hands-on.org/PFKB/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.hands-on.org/PFKB/</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||DJRice||10-08-1999||09:28 AM||||This is going to sound kind of bad, and we never tried it but this is an idea that myself and one of my friends dreamed up to make a deal on a car.<P>First, go look at the car, talk to the seller  , and indicate that he wants to much for the car and dont buy it.  (unless he comes off of the price)<P>Wait a day or so and have a friend offer the dude way less than what the car is worth.  The guy probably wont take it, but now he has had two hits and the car is still there...<P>Finally, you go back to take a second look at the car and offer more than what your friend did but less than what the guy wants for it.  <BR>Either you will get a good deal, the seller knows what the car is worth, or there is noone in the household who wants the car gone.<P>Like I said, we never tried this...but we figured this would work very well in the cases where someones wife or parents "just wants the car out of here."  After your friends low offer, the person who wants the car gone will be saying "He offered you how much?? and you didnt take it??  Cant we just get that car out of here??"<P>||||reg||1
Z||000012||batboy||10-08-1999||10:13 PM||||I've got some of my best deals on cars and trucks by waiting until the newspaper ad runs out and calling them. If the vehicle hasn't been sold, they are usually really ready to deal. On the other hand, if the ad is a new one, they usually are still very hopeful about getting their asking price and won't go down much, yet. I also get good deals by answering ads that require driving out into the country, because they can't get as many people to come out and look at the car.<P>One car I looked at once, I offered quite a bit less than he wanted, so the guy told me that another person was interested and had offered more than I had. I told him "ok, that's fine" and wrote my phone number down and told him that if his other deal fell through to call me if he wanted to sell it for what I offered. I drove home and walked into the door just as the phone rang. It was the guy saying he would take my offer. He was making up the story about the other person trying to get me to raise my original offer.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Monkeyman||11-20-1999||02:54 AM||||Tell everyone you know that you're looking for a GT.  Even if someone doesn't have any idea exactly what you're looking for, you'll still have the word out.  Just bought my 1st.  Not much to look at (yet!!), but found out about it through my wifes' ex-husband.  See, you CAN find leads in the strangest places.||||reg||1
Z||000014||GT Bastard||11-20-1999||03:58 PM||||Just because the car has been sitting, I wouldn't totally forget about it.  My '87 Gt was sitting for close to 7 months before I purchased it.  All it needed was a tune-up, a couple of tire fixes, and a couple of tranny coolant lines.  I've driven the car for 1500 miles and I've had no problems. ||||reg||1
Z||000015||mshill||11-22-1999||01:52 PM||||Three months ago when I bought my 86SE (in the Sacramento, CA area)  I had my choice of probably five cars (all V6s) for less than $1500 and three were less than $1000.  I keep checking the paper every Sunday for more deals and now there is a single 87GT in the paper and the guy wants $5000 for it.<P>When I got my 86SE, the guy wanted $950, but the ad ran out and I called him to see if it was still available and he said he would let it go for $750.  I bought it that day.  It has the obligatory drivers side seat tear, but other than that it is clean inside and out.<P>I did have to replace the radiator/hoses at $110, clutch master $8 (at boneyard) and catalytic convertor $100 to get it running and smogged.  Along the way I also replaced the distibutor pickup coil, cap/rotor, ignition module, wires and plugs to the tune of around $200.<P>Of course I couldn't stop there.  I have done the poly bushings, shocks, bearings, rotors and brake pads on the front end and have the poly bushings, struts, engine rebuild kit (including cam) for the backside. <P>After the engine and rear suspension is done I will have about $2500 into the car, but just about everything will be new.<P>My philosophy on buying 13-15 year old cars is this... "Pay as little as possible, because you will have to replace just about everything anyway."<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000016||sdm1964||11-24-1999||09:22 PM||||Keep the faith man, i got lucky with mine. The only fiero in the autotrader. It was an 85 Gt auto, power everything , new paint interior. Got it for 1,600. There are out there||||reg||6
Z||000017||geyser||11-24-1999||09:48 PM||||I got a great deal on my 86SE. The local fire dept was going to use it as extrication practice. They thought it was too small for good practice so they gave it to me. Needs a small amount of work, but all there and all the options.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Dan||11-25-1999||01:46 AM|||| Lots of good buys out there.In the least make sure the interior/exterior are in good condition.Check to see that the car wasn't all burgered up, electcical wires cut/spliced, try to find one that has been garaged.Many out there that were well taken care of and something happened and the owner decides to sell. Great opportunity to deal around Christmas time.My 86 SE sat for sale for six weeks when I bought it and in that time noone stopped to look it over.By the time I showed up he dropped the asking price<BR>from 1695 to 1195as bottom line.When you look it over,go top,bottom,inside and out.Even bring a compression tester and evualate what you are looking for.Right now most Fieros are BUYERS MARKRT.I find the worst people to buy from are women who had a long attachment to their Fiero.They seem mighty proud of them.Next worst group are teenagers.I guess the drive the shit out of them attitude and no mechanical aptitude takes its toll on old cars.Not that good cars can't be bought from them but be patient.Put an ad in your local trader,wanted<BR>Fiero for parts/restore and let the deals find you.Remember do you want to spend your time fixing or driving,the effort involved and what you will have when you finish.||||reg||1
